+        # A `/` survives Python's server-side SNI decoding -- verified
+        # 2026-09-01 -- so an unchecked servername of
+        # `../../../../etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates` makes FakeNet, running as
+        # root on the HYPERVISOR, truncate and rewrite that file. The write
+        # happens inside this callback, BEFORE the handshake fails on our
+        # untrusted certificate, so the `tls-untrusted-cert` caveat does not
+        # stand between a sample and the filesystem. `,` is a second hole in
+        # the same value: OpenSSL parses the subjectAltName string as a
+        # comma-separated GeneralName list, so a sample can append entries to
+        # the certificate it is served.
+        #
+        # A hostname is what this field is for, so admit a hostname and
+        # nothing else: ASCII letters, digits, dot, hyphen and underscore, no
+        # leading dot, within the DNS length limit. That admits every name a
+        # real sample resolves. An IDN arrives here already decoded from
+        # punycode and is the one legitimate value this turns away, at the
+        # cost of a certificate CN nothing trusts anyway.
+        #
